Confirmed layout:
- `out_buf = kmalloc(QAIC_MANAGE_MAX_MSG_LENGTH)` = kmalloc(4096)
- `struct wire_msg { struct wire_msg_hdr hdr (32 bytes); u8 data[]; } __packed`
- so data extends from offset 32 to 4095 → 4064 bytes accessible.

resp_worker orphan path:
```c
u32 msg_count = le32_to_cpu(msg->hdr.count);
u32 msg_len = le32_to_cpu(msg->hdr.len);
u32 len = 0;
int j;

for (j = 0; j < msg_count && len < msg_len; ++j) {
    struct wire_trans_hdr *trans_hdr;
    trans_hdr = (struct wire_trans_hdr *)(msg->data + len);
    if (le32_to_cpu(trans_hdr->type) == QAIC_TRANS_DEACTIVATE_FROM_DEV) {
        if (decode_deactivate(qdev, trans_hdr, &len, NULL))
            len += le32_to_cpu(trans_hdr->len);
    } else {
        len += le32_to_cpu(trans_hdr->len);
    }
}
kfree(msg);
```

Attack: device sends (post-timeout) response with hdr.magic valid, count=1, len=0xFFFFFFF0, and at msg->data[0] a trans hdr with type=QAIC_TRANS_DEACTIVATE_FROM_DEV, len=0x400 (1024) — first iteration: decode_deactivate reads in_trans->dbc_id (offset 8 in wire_trans_deactivate_from_dev) — in-bounds (data[8..11]); dbc_id < num_dbc assumed valid; status==0 → release_dbc(qdev, dbc_id) (a state-corruption effect: releases a DBC that a new user may be using! That's a use-after-free style state issue, but assume controlled); returns 0; then `len += le32_to_cpu(trans_hdr->len)` → len = 0x400 = 1024. Loop: j=1 < count? No — count=1, loop ends. OK make count = 12: len goes 1024, 2048, ... 3072, 4096 (> 4064 → OOB at 5th read at offset 4096+... within 4096-buffer? data[4096] is exactly at the end of the 4096 buffer → OOB read.

Note: decode_deactivate is only called when type == QAIC_TRANS_DEACTIVATE_FROM_DEV; the len advance for DEACTIVATE path is `if (decode_deactivate(...)) len += trans_hdr->len;` — wait, re-read:

```c
if (le32_to_cpu(trans_hdr->type) == QAIC_TRANS_DEACTIVATE_FROM_DEV) {
    if (decode_deactivate(qdev, trans_hdr, &len, NULL))
        len += le32_to_cpu(trans_hdr->len);
} else {
    len += le32_to_cpu(trans_hdr->len);
}
```

decode_deactivate returns 0 on success and advances *msg_len += sizeof(*in_trans) (24 bytes). On failure returns -ENODEV/-ECANCELED (nonzero) → then `len += trans_hdr->len` (peer value). So:

- DEACTIVATE with valid dbc_id and status 0: len += 24 (bounded advance, good).
- DEACTIVATE with invalid dbc_id (>= num_dbc): decode_deactivate returns -ENODEV without advancing len... then `if (decode_deactivate(...))` is TRUE (nonzero) → `len += le32_to_cpu(trans_hdr->len)` — arbitrary peer len! So even the "error" path advances by the arbitrary wire len.
- Non-DEACTIVATE type: len += trans_hdr->len directly — arbitrary peer len.

So any non-DEACTIVATE transaction (or failing DEACTIVATE) advances len by an arbitrary u32 wire value with no bound vs the 4096-byte buffer. The loop condition `len < msg_len` uses another arbitrary wire value; with msg_len = 0xFFFFFFFF and count = 0xFFFFFFFF, the loop keeps reading trans_hdr at msg->data + len as long as len keeps increasing — OOB reads far past the 4096 buffer (e.g., len steps of 0x400 → walks megabytes until fault or wraps).

Also note when len wraps u32 (e.g., trans_hdr->len = 0xFFFFFFF0 → len wraps to small), the loop can cycle within the buffer — infinite loop potential, or OOB reads.

Compare with the sibling request path (qaic_manage → qaic_manage_msg_xfer → decode_message): decode_message validates msg_hdr_len = le32_to_cpu(msg->hdr.len) with checks at lines ~975: `msg_hdr_len > QAIC_MANAGE_MAX_MSG_LENGTH` rejected; and per-transaction `size_add(msg_len, hdr_len) > msg_hdr_len` rejected (lines 989-993 in the older snapshot; need to verify in s07). The user-supplied request count is clamped at qaic_manage:1259-1260. So asymmetric validation: request path enforces MAX; orphan-response path (resp_worker) enforces nothing — count and len are raw wire values with no clamp vs QAIC_MANAGE_MAX_MSG_LENGTH or bytes_xferd.

This is the same finding family as f012 (reported in s01). In s07 the code is essentially identical (this is upstream qaic code that has never been fixed for this path — actually let me check if there's any upstream fix... The resp_worker orphan scan exists in upstream; I recall CVE-2023-49103? No, that's something else. There was a fix "accel/qaic: Add missing free_blob" etc. Let me check whether the loop got a bounds check in later upstream... I believe upstream still has this shape; there was commit c8d729554e3d "accel/qaic: Fix wire offsets..."? Not sure. Either way, in s07 the code as-is has the unclamped wire count/len consumption.

Let me verify decode_message's checks in s07 to document the enforcing sibling path.

Also for a finding I need: untrusted source (device/QSM via MHI control channel — peer of virtual bus protocol), violated invariant (no range check of count/len against known max or received size), unsafe use (loop reading trans headers at msg->data + len, OOB read; plus decode_deactivate dereferences trans at msg->data+len reading dbc_id/status OOB; also release_dbc on arbitrary valid dbc_id → state corruption).

Additionally the OOB read feeds decode_deactivate → release_dbc(qdev, dbc_id) where dbc_id is read OOB — a device can point len such that trans_hdr lands OOB where memory contains DEACTIVATE type... Actually simpler: the direct OOB read of trans_hdr->type at msg->data + len for len >= 4064 is itself the OOB. Then if that random memory equals DEACTIVATE type, decode_deactivate reads more OOB and calls release_dbc with OOB-read dbc_id — releasing a DBC in use → use-after-free of dbc resources. That's a strong consequence chain but speculative on memory contents.

The cleanest statement: count and len from wire, no clamp; loop walks msg->data + len for len up to msg_len (both attacker-set), reading past the 4096-byte kmalloc buffer.

